VALUES ('c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 'ccs-practice-1', 'CCS Practice Paper 1', 'ccs', ARRAY['ICD-10-CM', 'ICD-10-PCS', 'UHDDS', 'POA', 'DRG', 'Coding Documentation', 'Provider Queries', 'Regulatory Compliance', 'HIPAA', 'HIM Technology']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 1, 240);

-- ── Section: Domain 1 — Coding Knowledge and Skills (40 questions) — Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank. ──

VALUES ('49da9bce-40ce-40a8-9d20-c14f9df3d860', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 1, 'Per UHDDS, the principal diagnosis is defined as:');
VALUES ('49da9bce-40ce-40a8-9d20-c14f9df3d860', '2ad66089-36d1-40b7-9ed9-91d61a013151', 'UHDDS defines the principal diagnosis as the condition established AFTER STUDY to be chiefly responsible for occasioning the admission — not simply the first-listed condition or the most resource-intensive one.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('cb0c294f-68f9-4d1c-81ea-1906ba9b9aae', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 2, 'A patient is admitted with acute exacerbation of COPD and is also found to have an incidental, asymptomatic 4mm pulmonary nodule requiring no treatment during this stay. Which coding principle applies to the nodule?');
VALUES ('cb0c294f-68f9-4d1c-81ea-1906ba9b9aae', 'c89619f2-ede5-43e9-9a65-e601faf3bc4e', 'The Official Guidelines'' definition of ''additional diagnosis'' — not blanket ''code everything documented'' — governs whether a secondary condition is reportable; a truly incidental finding with no associated clinical significance/evaluation would not meet that definition.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('f65205b7-ead1-465b-860d-8e56c92d82d2', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 3, 'In ICD-10-PCS, the 3rd character of a Medical and Surgical section code represents:');
VALUES ('f65205b7-ead1-465b-860d-8e56c92d82d2', '13a2620a-037c-4244-9da1-bc8fe5be12e2', 'ICD-10-PCS Medical and Surgical section structure: 1=Section, 2=Body System, 3=Root Operation, 4=Body Part, 5=Approach, 6=Device, 7=Qualifier.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('bb7bd63a-7947-46e3-81f6-f69d4e24ec49', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 4, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as ''cutting out or off, without replacement, a portion of a body part''?');
VALUES ('bb7bd63a-7947-46e3-81f6-f69d4e24ec49', '22672aaa-efa3-41b1-8cf9-c54e15ba8b7f', 'Excision = cutting out/off a PORTION of a body part without replacement. Resection is the same definition but for the ENTIRE body part. Extraction is pulling/stripping out. Destruction is eradicating without taking any of it out.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('97c79cc6-a331-43f3-b1a6-86762438efe5', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 5, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as ''cutting out or off, without replacement, ALL of a body part''?');
VALUES ('97c79cc6-a331-43f3-b1a6-86762438efe5', '451daf17-dc92-498b-b3a7-5cf711c4e636', 'Resection removes the entire body part; Excision removes only a portion — this distinction (partial vs. entire) is one of the most heavily tested root-operation pairs on the CCS exam.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5912281d-5f0c-4757-b2c4-6ae690a2f88d', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 6, 'A patient undergoes a procedure to restore, to the extent possible, a body part to its normal anatomic structure and function after it was damaged by disease or trauma. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation applies?');
VALUES ('5912281d-5f0c-4757-b2c4-6ae690a2f88d', '6f37a798-5a38-4328-98a5-29511a1b6f3d', 'Repair is the ''not elsewhere classified'' root operation for restoring anatomic structure/function using methods other than the other specifically-defined root operations.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5a2181fe-288c-456e-801c-680556fcacc9', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 7, 'Present on Admission (POA) indicator ''W'' means:');
VALUES ('5a2181fe-288c-456e-801c-680556fcacc9', 'beacda9a-b42f-44ad-ba4d-9ee4594b67b4', 'POA indicators: Y=present on admission, N=not present on admission, U=documentation insufficient to determine, W=clinically undetermined (provider unable to determine whether present on admission), 1=exempt from POA reporting.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('1655cf08-16ec-44b5-b2f6-9b2c572c04e9', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 8, 'A condition is documented as ''possible'' pneumonia at the time of discharge from an inpatient stay, with no more definitive diagnosis ever established. Per the Official Guidelines for inpatient coding, this should be:');
VALUES ('1655cf08-16ec-44b5-b2f6-9b2c572c04e9', 'f1758acd-4bc8-4842-ad6e-bdce4c3f46a2', 'The inpatient-only ''uncertain diagnosis'' guideline allows coding a possible/probable/suspected condition documented at discharge as if confirmed — this guideline does NOT apply to outpatient coding, which is a frequently tested contrast.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('8874da51-fce9-4f69-b7b7-ee302bbd3d84', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 9, 'Which of the following is the correct treatment of an uncertain diagnosis (''rule out appendicitis'') in the OUTPATIENT setting, per ICD-10-CM guidelines?');
VALUES ('8874da51-fce9-4f69-b7b7-ee302bbd3d84', '7c9043b8-506a-4620-b085-a5daee6c06b3', 'Outpatient/ambulatory guidelines are the opposite of the inpatient uncertain-diagnosis rule: do not code conditions described as probable/suspected/rule-out — code to the highest degree of certainty, i.e., the signs and symptoms.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('8edeeb89-9439-467a-93de-9d339ba206ac', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 10, 'A Major Complication/Comorbidity (MCC) or Complication/Comorbidity (CC), when accurately captured through coding, primarily affects:');
VALUES ('8edeeb89-9439-467a-93de-9d339ba206ac', 'aeba0aaf-80ba-4c78-9496-019f56ef4a6e', 'CC/MCC capture is the mechanism by which MS-DRG assignment reflects severity/resource intensity — an MCC generally moves a case into a higher-weighted DRG tier than a CC, and no CC/MCC into the base DRG.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('ce8ef9e7-163b-4722-b7da-b9a7ed8593ad', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 11, 'For a patient with diabetes mellitus and a documented diabetic condition (e.g., diabetic nephropathy), current ICD-10-CM guidelines require:');
VALUES ('ce8ef9e7-163b-4722-b7da-b9a7ed8593ad', '8290f83c-e21c-4137-8057-095a0ebd7736', 'ICD-10-CM''s diabetes codes are combination codes; per the guidelines, the word ''with'' in the Alphabetic Index should be interpreted as a link between diabetes and the listed condition even without a physician explicitly stating a causal relationship, unless the documentation states the conditions are unrelated.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('cea0a5e5-805b-4f10-b2c6-324ee355da21', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 12, 'A patient is admitted for surgical treatment of a condition, but during the same admission develops an unrelated acute condition requiring equal or more intensive care. Under UHDDS principal-diagnosis selection rules, when two or more conditions meet the definition for principal diagnosis, coding guidance directs:');
VALUES ('cea0a5e5-805b-4f10-b2c6-324ee355da21', 'c6a919fd-73bb-4429-89a4-83817395597a', 'When two or more diagnoses equally meet the definition of principal diagnosis, the guidelines allow sequencing either first UNLESS the circumstances of the admission, diagnostic workup, and/or therapy indicate a different principal diagnosis is more appropriate.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('80dd3b1f-9211-47c6-9aa6-781d8b989155', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 13, 'Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-PCS root operation ''Control''?');
VALUES ('80dd3b1f-9211-47c6-9aa6-781d8b989155', '28beae12-a89e-493a-a9eb-4adf9725b2b0', 'Control is specifically for stopping/attempting to stop post-procedural bleeding, distinct from Repair (which would be used if control of bleeding required more than simple techniques, e.g., suturing a bleeding vessel).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('261db629-3223-40b7-b02b-1150e3c846be', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 14, 'A patient has a laparoscopic cholecystectomy that is converted to an open procedure due to complications. Per ICD-10-PCS coding conventions for converted procedures, the approach coded is:');
VALUES ('261db629-3223-40b7-b02b-1150e3c846be', 'd4a4cd50-7538-4b45-8bd8-064aa9754807', 'When an approach is converted, ICD-10-PCS conventions direct coding the approach that was ultimately used to complete the procedure, not an approach that was attempted and converted away from.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('57760db8-6810-40ef-81d2-8f5af63547d8', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 15, 'Which root operation involves ''putting in a device that monitors, assists, performs, or prevents a physiological function but does not physically take the place of a body part''?');
VALUES ('57760db8-6810-40ef-81d2-8f5af63547d8', '9aad2ad9-4cf0-4a31-be96-a919afe11434', 'Insertion = putting in a nonbiological device that monitors/assists/performs/prevents a function without replacing a body part (e.g., pacemaker lead). Replacement physically takes the place of a body part; Supplement reinforces/augments a body part with a device or autologous/nonautologous material.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('d058ac88-edea-4a48-a4f5-c9d525e2c6a3', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 16, 'A hospital''s coding staff must determine whether a diagnosis is an ''other/secondary diagnosis'' for UHDDS reporting purposes. Which of the following would NOT independently qualify a condition as a reportable secondary diagnosis?');
VALUES ('d058ac88-edea-4a48-a4f5-c9d525e2c6a3', 'ffb0489f-4f67-444f-b286-8d121cca77b6', 'A condition that is merely documented in history with zero clinical evaluation, treatment, extended stay, or monitoring impact does not meet UHDDS''s ''additional diagnosis'' definition and should not automatically be coded as a secondary diagnosis.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a4d6e3a5-0141-47ef-a5f7-285f2b28e336', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 17, 'For a two-stage bilateral joint replacement performed during the same operative episode on both the right and left knee, ICD-10-PCS coding convention requires:');
VALUES ('a4d6e3a5-0141-47ef-a5f7-285f2b28e336', '727cc92b-c022-455e-8950-7736c0922033', 'ICD-10-PCS body part values are generally side-specific (right/left); a bilateral procedure on paired body parts each having distinct body part values requires a separate procedure code for each side, per the multiple-procedures coding convention.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('08bdfb69-e6e1-410b-a237-f21885fb3c01', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 18, 'In ICD-10-PCS, which root operation is defined as ''taking out solid matter from a body part''?');
VALUES ('08bdfb69-e6e1-410b-a237-f21885fb3c01', '01ea10cd-a303-445d-9b4d-ef587341a8d5', 'Extraction = pulling or stripping out (by force) all or a portion of a body part, using the force of the extraction to separate the portion being taken out (e.g., D&C, vein stripping, tooth extraction).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('564f7ec9-736f-4947-9589-0f700244200f', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 19, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation means ''taking out or eliminating all or a portion of a body part by the use of force to separate or cut off the body part'' via extreme cold, chemicals, or radiofrequency energy rather than mechanical means?');
VALUES ('564f7ec9-736f-4947-9589-0f700244200f', '0af8f15c-a2d2-462f-bf56-7941d6cd899f', 'Destruction physically eradicates body part tissue by direct application of energy (e.g., cryoablation, radiofrequency ablation, fulguration) with no part of the body part being physically taken out — distinguishing it from Excision/Resection.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3e6b282f-a027-4ac4-9710-f2a82e9e098a', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 20, 'According to the Official Guidelines, when a patient is admitted for a condition and, after study, that condition is determined not to require further care, but a comorbidity discovered during the stay ends up requiring the majority of the workup and treatment, the principal diagnosis is:');
VALUES ('3e6b282f-a027-4ac4-9710-f2a82e9e098a', '4bde34c2-262d-47d8-b4b6-f01cf1512838', 'Resource intensity during the stay is not the UHDDS test for principal diagnosis — the reason the admission itself was occasioned, established after study, governs, even if a different condition consumed more care once hospitalized.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('23f47992-0394-40a5-8412-fec0512db468', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 21, 'A ''combination code'' in ICD-10-CM is best described as:');
VALUES ('23f47992-0394-40a5-8412-fec0512db468', 'cb37b34c-e62e-4d90-9f8d-9532242e50ec', 'Combination codes classify two diagnoses together, a diagnosis with an associated manifestation, or a diagnosis with an associated complication, all within one code — common in conditions like diabetes with manifestations.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('12ea3131-66f4-473d-8ef3-d402ea7fb8f6', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 22, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as ''moving to its normal location, or other suitable location, all or a portion of a body part''?');
VALUES ('12ea3131-66f4-473d-8ef3-d402ea7fb8f6', '568ea302-82f0-43b9-ba2d-a1f69b10de2e', 'Reposition moves a body part to a normal or other suitable location without taking out any of the body part (e.g., reducing a fracture, repositioning an undescended testicle).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a546e302-acb8-40d7-a7ac-24dc4fadac31', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 23, 'A patient is coded with a 7th character extension of ''S'' on an injury code. What does this extension represent?');
VALUES ('a546e302-acb8-40d7-a7ac-24dc4fadac31', 'ea13e3dd-e8c4-4e28-af85-7d7a4524116e', 'For injury, poisoning, and certain other codes requiring 7th character extensions: A=initial encounter, D=subsequent encounter, S=sequela (a condition that is the residual effect after the acute phase of an illness/injury has ended).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('547db636-06cd-4a52-a0c0-c3085901a868', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 24, 'The ''excludes1'' note in ICD-10-CM means:');
VALUES ('547db636-06cd-4a52-a0c0-c3085901a868', '630491eb-22fe-4d38-b78c-bfd4f7e1751d', 'Excludes1 = ''not coded here'' — the two conditions cannot be reported together because they aren''t clinically possible to coexist as classified (e.g., a code and its congenital version). Excludes2 means ''not included here'' — the excluded condition IS permitted to be coded together with the other.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('4db8b990-b776-4e9a-9345-f13aac468f5a', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 25, 'For DRG-based inpatient reimbursement, which of the following most directly determines whether a case groups to a higher-weighted MS-DRG within the same base DRG family?');
VALUES ('4db8b990-b776-4e9a-9345-f13aac468f5a', 'f22c36cc-209e-406d-8724-ef69943fc1fb', 'MS-DRGs are typically structured in three severity tiers per base DRG (with MCC, with CC, without CC/MCC) — accurately capturing a qualifying CC/MCC secondary diagnosis is what moves a case to a higher-weighted tier, not raw code count or documentation length.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3a4ed692-288c-4ade-a23b-e708b8c8a01d', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 26, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation describes ''putting back in, or taking out and putting back in, all or a portion of a separated body part to its normal location or other suitable location''?');
VALUES ('3a4ed692-288c-4ade-a23b-e708b8c8a01d', '1a922d1f-1253-4de3-92a3-5bbfa71d3917', 'Reattachment reconnects a separated body part (e.g., a severed finger) — distinct from Reposition, which moves a body part that was never separated from the body.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('73b99b43-5054-4f4c-ab13-da9d8c40c8a4', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 27, 'A patient''s ICD-10-PCS procedure involves correcting, to the extent possible, a portion of a malfunctioning device without taking out and replacing the entire device. Which root operation applies?');
VALUES ('73b99b43-5054-4f4c-ab13-da9d8c40c8a4', 'fcec27ba-199f-4ad6-8546-cf5b79b92a13', 'Revision corrects, to the extent possible, a malfunctioning or displaced device — Removal takes a device out with no replacement, Change exchanges a device without cutting/puncturing, and Replacement physically substitutes a body part.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('e05a83b2-9b0b-459f-89be-760a496d938b', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 28, 'In ICD-10-CM sequencing, when a patient is admitted specifically for the management of a malignancy (e.g., chemotherapy) and the treatment is the sole reason for admission, the principal diagnosis is typically:');
VALUES ('e05a83b2-9b0b-459f-89be-760a496d938b', '3d9cba35-0a46-4b28-8df9-0e67c77c45f1', 'Per the ICD-10-CM neoplasm guidelines, when the encounter is solely for administration of chemotherapy/immunotherapy/radiation therapy, the appropriate Z code for the encounter is sequenced first, with the malignancy coded as an additional diagnosis.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('08af2d67-e4d7-4f68-90f2-d4f63b213766', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 29, 'The term ''laterality'' in ICD-10-CM/PCS refers to:');
VALUES ('08af2d67-e4d7-4f68-90f2-d4f63b213766', 'ee68d19f-b66c-4112-91c9-7ceabcacc5e6', 'Laterality specifies right/left/bilateral for paired organs and body parts, and unspecified-laterality codes should only be used when the medical record genuinely does not document laterality — not as a default convenience.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('8b561641-f6ba-4f87-a9d5-c17e77fa9e24', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 30, 'A discharge summary documents ''sepsis due to E. coli UTI, resulting in severe sepsis with acute kidney injury.'' Per the sepsis coding guidelines, the coding of severe sepsis requires:');
VALUES ('8b561641-f6ba-4f87-a9d5-c17e77fa9e24', '963080b9-cfcb-4ba1-b84e-c46b61ad6375', 'Severe sepsis coding requires (at minimum) the underlying infection code, a code from the R65.2- severe sepsis subcategory, and code(s) for the specific associated acute organ dysfunction(s) — omitting the organ dysfunction code is a common real-world coding error.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a3d2fc20-8c1e-4f09-81e1-db1271e65b09', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 31, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation means ''altering the natural anatomic structure of a body part without taking out or replacing any of the body part, to restrict or narrow the diameter/orifice''?');
VALUES ('a3d2fc20-8c1e-4f09-81e1-db1271e65b09', '623e246b-34c3-4cbb-9cf3-87a07de34154', 'Restriction partially narrows the diameter/orifice of a tubular body part (e.g., gastric banding); Occlusion completely closes it off; Dilation expands/widens it.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('cbd412ae-24ef-419a-a58c-bf0bf6920b87', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 32, 'Which of the following correctly distinguishes ''Occlusion'' from ''Restriction'' in ICD-10-PCS?');
VALUES ('cbd412ae-24ef-419a-a58c-bf0bf6920b87', '7c5b60fa-7810-4028-9b8d-bc7cd2e61ced', 'Occlusion completely closes an orifice/lumen (e.g., clipping an aneurysm neck completely); Restriction only partially narrows it (e.g., a restrictive band). Both may or may not use a device.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('c8a76014-861d-43a9-94cf-173be2f1f72d', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 33, 'A coder assigns codes for an encounter documenting ''chronic kidney disease, stage 3, due to hypertension.'' ICD-10-CM''s hypertensive chronic kidney disease combination code assumes a causal relationship between hypertension and CKD:');
VALUES ('c8a76014-861d-43a9-94cf-173be2f1f72d', '889e1362-ed74-47a4-9293-fc5ebbb93403', 'Per ICD-10-CM guidelines, a causal relationship between hypertension and CKD is presumed by classification, and both conditions are coded as related even without explicit provider linkage — unless documentation clearly indicates they are unrelated.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('8d316f10-3691-4a0c-92eb-42ea855c6b63', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 34, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as ''putting in a biological or synthetic material that physically takes the place of all or a portion of a body part''?');
VALUES ('8d316f10-3691-4a0c-92eb-42ea855c6b63', '49e0f7c0-3cd1-48e1-bc47-1d705e1a6b7d', 'Replacement physically takes the place of a body part (autograft, allograft, or synthetic substitute, e.g., total knee arthroplasty) — the original body part may or may not be physically taken out.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('f28dc650-852b-41ea-b57a-44bd9129167b', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 35, 'A malignant neoplasm has metastasized from its primary site to a secondary site. When both the primary and secondary sites require treatment during an encounter, and the reason for the encounter is treatment of the metastatic (secondary) site, sequencing is:');
VALUES ('f28dc650-852b-41ea-b57a-44bd9129167b', '6cb316dc-5b66-44bd-9602-31d84b248aa5', 'The neoplasm coding guideline directs sequencing based on which site (primary or secondary) is the reason for the current encounter/treatment focus, not automatically defaulting to the primary site.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('1ad42fd5-e912-4070-aa47-aad7b6135c94', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 36, 'A postoperative complication code should be assigned only when:');
VALUES ('1ad42fd5-e912-4070-aa47-aad7b6135c94', '47a4febc-abd0-4682-aeaf-1cbb09c428c5', 'Coding a condition as a postprocedural/postoperative complication requires explicit provider documentation establishing that relationship — coders may not infer a cause-and-effect link from timing or proximity alone.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('4b4c285f-00df-4961-996e-74cc1ab48fed', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 37, 'Which of the following ICD-10-PCS Medical and Surgical root operations always involves taking out all of a body part?');
VALUES ('4b4c285f-00df-4961-996e-74cc1ab48fed', 'e0cb9a80-d844-4c55-bedd-64b72dac0832', 'Resection is defined as cutting out/off, without replacement, ALL of a body part — the defining feature distinguishing it from Excision (a portion).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('33be2956-0c48-41a3-ae0d-bd42398b280e', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 38, 'In the inpatient setting, if a patient''s final diagnosis at discharge includes a condition documented only as ''suspected,'' and the coder is uncertain whether it should be coded as confirmed, the most appropriate first action per AHIMA''s Standards of Ethical Coding is:');
VALUES ('33be2956-0c48-41a3-ae0d-bd42398b280e', '18fa21a7-d1b0-46a5-baad-727dde1b25de', 'While the inpatient uncertain-diagnosis guideline does permit coding ''possible/probable/suspected'' conditions as if confirmed, ethical coding practice still calls for querying when documentation is genuinely ambiguous or when the coder cannot confidently apply the guideline to the specific clinical picture — coding should never be based on assumption when a compliant query is a reasonable option.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('6dbd0de8-de6c-463f-8231-be3e54db23e4', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 39, 'A ''Z code'' in ICD-10-CM is generally used to report:');
VALUES ('6dbd0de8-de6c-463f-8231-be3e54db23e4', '8b1b109e-ae42-4afc-a967-3f53a0ef6723', 'Z codes (Factors Influencing Health Status and Contact with Health Services) cover encounters not primarily driven by acute illness/injury — health maintenance visits, screening, aftercare, personal/family history, and similar circumstances.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('850d93dc-2105-4764-8e6d-9cc4a946799d', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 40, 'A patient is admitted through the Emergency Department in septic shock secondary to a urinary tract infection. Per the sepsis sequencing guideline, the appropriate principal diagnosis is generally:');
VALUES ('850d93dc-2105-4764-8e6d-9cc4a946799d', 'ea5cfaef-f618-497d-9992-002e72ac4121', 'Sepsis sequencing requires the underlying systemic infection first, followed by the R65.2- severe sepsis code, with septic shock (a form of acute organ dysfunction) coded as well — septic shock is never sequenced as principal, and shock is always coded in addition to, not instead of, the underlying sepsis/infection.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

-- ── Section: Domain 2 — Coding Documentation (20 questions) — Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance. ──

VALUES ('00189343-0aa4-4b71-84cb-58881c3b290a', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 41, 'A health record documents ''right leg'' in the admission history but ''left leg'' in the operative report for the same procedure/injury. This is an example of:');
VALUES ('00189343-0aa4-4b71-84cb-58881c3b290a', 'd24188ba-7cd5-4cb6-a382-031df742d4d6', 'Genuinely conflicting documentation about a clinically significant detail like laterality cannot simply be guessed at or defaulted — it should be resolved, typically through querying the responsible provider(s), before finalizing the code.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('a69621b7-d104-4924-9968-739f34e1d9ff', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 42, 'For a code to be validly assigned, required supporting documentation must generally be located:');
VALUES ('a69621b7-d104-4924-9968-739f34e1d9ff', 'e45a9a26-e56a-4883-ad35-3bfbc111d4bf', 'Codeable diagnoses generally must be documented by a provider legally responsible for the diagnosis/care, within the health record for that encounter — a nurse''s or ancillary staff note alone typically cannot establish a new diagnosis for coding purposes.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('5b5d0874-07b3-428a-bec0-0a789b744c79', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 43, 'Which of the following is the best example of ''verifying and validating documentation'' before finalizing a code assignment?');
VALUES ('5b5d0874-07b3-428a-bec0-0a789b744c79', 'dcf2a861-0007-44e7-840f-16cd45bc589a', 'Validating documentation means confirming that a diagnosis/procedure is consistently and adequately supported across the record — not coding mechanically from a summary sheet or list without underlying clinical corroboration.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('690239a3-1969-433c-aceb-8027020742f6', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 44, 'A physician documents ''CHF'' in a progress note but the type (systolic, diastolic, acute, chronic) is never specified anywhere else in the record. For accurate, specific ICD-10-CM code assignment, the coder should:');
VALUES ('690239a3-1969-433c-aceb-8027020742f6', 'eef91de8-1d9f-47e6-8cca-c467d0492cd5', 'Coders cannot infer clinical specificity that isn''t documented — the choice is either an unspecified code (if no further information is obtainable) or a compliant query to the provider for clarification, never guessing a specific subtype.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('6a13a0ca-2312-4031-9c3a-e0c52520ea06', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 45, 'Which of the following scenarios represents a documentation completeness issue that would prevent confident ICD-10-PCS procedure code assignment?');
VALUES ('6a13a0ca-2312-4031-9c3a-e0c52520ea06', '63c30f88-d91e-4d7c-833d-4ac1a692cc1a', 'Without an operative report or equivalent documentation of what was actually done, a coder has no valid basis to assign a specific procedure code — a preoperative order describing an intended/possible procedure is not evidence of what was actually performed.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('4fdb1fc1-a58b-4a35-bfdf-7abfacd8869d', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 46, 'A discrepancy exists between the admission type recorded in the registration system (elective) and clinical documentation suggesting the patient was actually admitted emergently from the ED. This type of conflict falls under which documentation-integrity task?');
VALUES ('4fdb1fc1-a58b-4a35-bfdf-7abfacd8869d', '470f01e1-0451-4ccd-94e5-4507195f09ff', 'This is a direct example of ''resolve conflicting documentation in the health record (e.g., admission type, laterality)'' — one of the explicitly listed CCS Domain 2 tasks.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('531959d6-6d30-456f-a477-41d3b52aa3db', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 47, 'Verifying that documentation supports a reported present-on-admission indicator most directly relies on:');
VALUES ('531959d6-6d30-456f-a477-41d3b52aa3db', '61ce8bd1-8dcf-4d95-9d02-e197ae30fde9', 'POA determination is a clinical documentation question — whether the condition was present at the time of the order for inpatient admission — and requires clear clinical documentation of onset timing, not administrative or patient-reported data alone.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('695e0db2-f8ff-445e-bb07-ff28236fd02d', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 48, 'A coder notices that a diagnosis appears on the coding summary but nowhere else in the actual health record narrative, labs, orders, or notes. The appropriate action is:');
VALUES ('695e0db2-f8ff-445e-bb07-ff28236fd02d', 'fbebafa8-bb7b-42f4-968c-03bb47de98ea', 'Coding must be based on documentation within the health record itself, not solely a coding/charge summary sheet — an unsupported entry should prompt verification against the full record or a compliant query, not automatic code assignment.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('703991f9-bd56-4bf6-a241-8fc322f78bac', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 49, 'In a scenario where two attending physicians document different, non-conflicting but individually incomplete pieces of the clinical picture (e.g., one documents the diagnosis, another documents the treatment plan for it), the coder should:');
VALUES ('703991f9-bd56-4bf6-a241-8fc322f78bac', '599e66b3-d265-4bb1-a05d-d9ab5039a36c', 'Coding is based on the health record as a whole; complementary (non-conflicting) documentation from multiple legitimate providers can together support accurate code assignment without requiring one single all-encompassing note.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('1d2c2d69-aa86-444d-b342-c1dd8a06d43c', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 50, 'Which best describes ''required documentation elements'' for assigning a diagnosis code with a comorbidity/complication (CC/MCC) designation?');
VALUES ('1d2c2d69-aa86-444d-b342-c1dd8a06d43c', '0180999d-3a95-4ba3-b48f-17a6758bebea', 'Because CC/MCC-designated diagnoses can materially affect DRG assignment and reimbursement, they carry heightened documentation-integrity expectations — the condition needs credible clinical support (evaluation, treatment, monitoring) consistent with UHDDS''s additional-diagnosis definition, not a single unsupported mention.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('6e3ca389-8316-4c02-a340-c932e9563e50', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 51, 'A clinical documentation improvement (CDI) specialist flags a record where the H&P states ''malnutrition'' but there is no documented BMI, weight loss percentage, or nutritional assessment supporting a specific severity of malnutrition. From a documentation-integrity standpoint, this is:');
VALUES ('6e3ca389-8316-4c02-a340-c932e9563e50', '6ce2002e-f20f-4c49-a48b-a4a75d57c6dd', 'Severity-specific diagnoses like malnutrition staging require supporting clinical indicators consistent with recognized criteria; absent that support, the gap should be closed via additional documentation or a compliant query, not defaulted to the most severe available code.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('cd7d92ba-4303-4b84-b9ff-71b6a68de47e', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 52, 'Which of the following is an example of appropriately ''ensuring all required documentation for assigning a specified code is available within the body of the health record''?');
VALUES ('cd7d92ba-4303-4b84-b9ff-71b6a68de47e', '62ae6a6e-a611-4dd1-8002-de338aeb0564', 'This task is specifically about confirming that supporting documentation actually exists in the record for any code being assigned — most critically important, though not exclusively, for diagnoses that affect DRG/severity weighting.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('e4da2a69-8d28-4e76-a343-7118dc620161', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 53, 'A discharge summary states a patient has ''history of MI'' with no date given, while a cardiology consult note from the same admission documents an ''acute STEMI'' actively being treated during this stay. The correct interpretation is:');
VALUES ('e4da2a69-8d28-4e76-a343-7118dc620161', '2b1ed331-1f91-49c3-a6be-f9b679702d11', 'This is a documentation-consistency scenario: it''s plausible both are true and non-conflicting (old MI in history + a new acute MI this admission), but the coder must review carefully — collapsing them incorrectly, or ignoring a genuine ambiguity, both risk an inaccurate code assignment.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('6af54dd6-d015-4eea-a05f-d375c7de1fda', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 54, 'Documentation validation for procedure coding should confirm that the operative report is:');
VALUES ('6af54dd6-d015-4eea-a05f-d375c7de1fda', '6ca9db3b-4566-4f7d-970f-b7fcd0d4483f', 'Valid procedure documentation should be properly authenticated by the performing provider and internally consistent with other documentation of the same episode — inconsistency across these sources is a red flag requiring further review.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('abf363c7-10ed-46b3-b59d-98d68ccf5531', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 55, 'Which scenario best illustrates a documentation conflict that specifically affects POA determination (not just diagnosis specificity)?');
VALUES ('abf363c7-10ed-46b3-b59d-98d68ccf5531', '2e00e096-4742-4969-97c5-49dd31f7a22b', 'Without any admission-time documentation of skin condition, whether a pressure ulcer identified later was present on admission is genuinely unclear — exactly the kind of conflict/gap that affects POA assignment and often requires a query or review of nursing admission assessments.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c7f6883f-b051-4640-933e-335869d967df', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 56, 'A coder is assigning a procedure code from an operative report that describes the surgeon converting from a planned laparoscopic approach to open, but the report never explicitly restates the final approach in its closing summary line. The correct documentation-review practice is:');
VALUES ('c7f6883f-b051-4640-933e-335869d967df', 'aa29bbde-35ae-4b40-8e6e-d1ea53a66781', 'Coders must review the full narrative of an operative report, not rely on a potentially incomplete summary line, to accurately determine what approach and technique were actually completed — this is a core documentation-review competency, distinct from simply defaulting based on partial information.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('a3d83877-0335-49d9-b2fa-7ef363f9cd89', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 57, 'A nursing intake assessment documents a decubitus ulcer at admission, but no physician note anywhere in the record mentions it, and no treatment/monitoring for it is documented by any provider. From a coding-documentation standpoint, this is best handled by:');
VALUES ('a3d83877-0335-49d9-b2fa-7ef363f9cd89', '02b15f0b-99d8-47cb-acd9-195294121795', 'While nursing documentation (e.g., wound assessments) is a valid input for clinical facts like staging or POA determination once a diagnosis exists, the diagnosis itself generally still needs physician/qualified-provider documentation to be codeable — an isolated nursing note with no physician corroboration is a documentation gap to close via query or further chart review.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('bacd653e-cc5f-441d-873c-a4f718e8f1a1', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 58, 'Which of the following best reflects proper handling of an amended or corrected note in the health record for coding purposes?');
VALUES ('bacd653e-cc5f-441d-873c-a4f718e8f1a1', 'b6fc929b-4509-41fe-b2c3-b21202e00ae7', 'Proper amendments/addenda, made following an organization''s compliant correction process, become part of the legal health record and should be used for coding — the goal is coding from the most accurate, current documentation, not artificially freezing on an outdated or erroneous original entry.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('057f6a00-6f7b-4cb7-97bf-4eb77a550a80', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 59, 'A discharge summary and an ED physician note disagree on whether a patient''s altered mental status was due to hypoglycemia or a new stroke, with no later note in the record resolving which was accurate. This scenario requires:');
VALUES ('057f6a00-6f7b-4cb7-97bf-4eb77a550a80', 'bd4f6086-18f1-4fab-b0c0-545eeff1e4b3', 'Truly unresolved clinical disagreement about the actual diagnosis (not just a wording or administrative discrepancy) is a documentation-integrity issue requiring resolution — via further record review or a compliant query — rather than an arbitrary coder choice between conflicting provider opinions.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('b2acfbe0-31db-40de-8095-5ebd3cc2d0c9', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 60, 'A coder abstracting data for UHDDS reporting notices that the documented discharge disposition (e.g., ''discharged to home'' vs. ''discharged to skilled nursing facility'') differs between the case management note and the physician''s discharge order. Because discharge disposition affects both UHDDS reporting and MS-DRG post-acute transfer payment policy, the coder should:');
VALUES ('b2acfbe0-31db-40de-8095-5ebd3cc2d0c9', 'f211afe6-5727-4444-a7e4-69661cd0fb0f', 'Discharge disposition is a UHDDS data element with direct payment consequences (post-acute-care transfer policy can reduce payment for certain MS-DRGs when disposition is to another facility rather than home) — a genuine conflict here must be resolved from the record, not assumed.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

-- ── Section: Domain 3 — Provider Queries (10 questions) — Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles). ──

VALUES ('56ce4eaf-46ce-49c0-96bf-0509b14707bb', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 61, 'Which of the following is a core element of an ethical, compliant provider query?');
VALUES ('56ce4eaf-46ce-49c0-96bf-0509b14707bb', '0c3c3c8c-1845-4121-80f3-0d00f2fc65c9', 'A compliant query must be non-leading: it presents relevant clinical indicators and reasonable, clinically plausible options without suggesting a specific diagnosis the provider is expected to simply confirm.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('f156a414-44a1-4177-ad6e-5fb13a459307', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 62, 'A query stating: ''Since the patient''s creatinine rose to 2.1 from a baseline of 0.9 during the admission, please confirm the diagnosis of acute kidney injury'' is problematic because:');
VALUES ('f156a414-44a1-4177-ad6e-5fb13a459307', '8282ead0-206e-4d8b-a843-1068126bfe65', 'Directly telling the provider what diagnosis to ''confirm'' is a leading query — a compliant version would present the clinical indicators (creatinine trend, baseline) and ask the provider to clarify the clinical significance/diagnosis, if any, without presupposing the answer.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('50b5848c-7a78-42e4-8766-211e12b3f04f', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 63, 'Which of the following would generally be considered an appropriate, non-leading way to query for potential acute kidney injury based on the same creatinine trend?');
VALUES ('50b5848c-7a78-42e4-8766-211e12b3f04f', '494ccc1e-5355-4973-9350-d539cb414fff', 'This version presents the clinical indicator objectively and offers multiple genuine clinical possibilities (including ''not clinically significant''), letting the provider exercise independent clinical judgment — the hallmark of a compliant query.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('36116927-cafd-4c72-8dbb-3e47440ad726', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 64, 'Identifying ''query opportunities'' by analyzing current documentation primarily means:');
VALUES ('36116927-cafd-4c72-8dbb-3e47440ad726', '8131c50a-8b14-42b8-95f2-2b4751ac6331', 'Query opportunity identification is about spotting a genuine mismatch between clinical evidence and documented specificity/completeness that materially affects coding — not queries for their own sake or purely administrative gaps.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('108617df-c3bb-40a7-8b3e-f79f0a8f3b28', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 65, 'A compliant query may appropriately include which of the following as an answer option?');
VALUES ('108617df-c3bb-40a7-8b3e-f79f0a8f3b28', 'ad3c43d4-86ef-484f-9f29-8122746e884d', 'Including options like ''clinically undetermined'' (in addition to reasonable clinical alternatives) helps keep a multiple-choice-format query non-leading, since the provider is not forced into confirming a specific suspected diagnosis.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('fd0206fd-a3d0-4a26-aacf-7f1a96cdcd0a', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 66, 'Under a compliance-driven query program, verbal (non-written) queries are generally considered acceptable only when:');
VALUES ('fd0206fd-a3d0-4a26-aacf-7f1a96cdcd0a', 'ae4e52ff-b2b0-4f81-92b9-9adf74196cf9', 'Regardless of format, sound query practice calls for the query and its resolution to be reflected/documented per organizational policy, preserving an auditable trail — an undocumented, purely verbal exchange undermines compliance and record completeness.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('1302b9ee-afde-4705-a616-96c4b061028e', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 67, 'Which of the following queries would most likely be considered leading and non-compliant?');
VALUES ('1302b9ee-afde-4705-a616-96c4b061028e', 'bf720bae-e97f-4be7-92aa-f1ee22c76795', 'Explicitly tying the requested diagnosis to a reimbursement/DRG outcome, and asking the provider to simply ''confirm'' a specific diagnosis, are both hallmarks of a leading, non-compliant query.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('63ef0f7e-f057-4b1b-b6f2-5d76d786cc6e', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 68, 'A key reason ethical query compliance matters for CCS-credentialed coders specifically is:');
VALUES ('63ef0f7e-f057-4b1b-b6f2-5d76d786cc6e', '09514ea2-c9c0-464a-806b-c09f8b925fc9', 'Because queries directly shape what gets documented and subsequently coded, leading queries create real compliance and fraud-exposure risk — this is why AHIMA''s ethical coding standards and query-practice guidance treat non-leading query construction as a core coder competency.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('fdfb2293-b0f9-4b99-aa1a-d780d6ed93ab', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 69, 'Analyzing documentation for query opportunities related to CC/MCC capture would most appropriately flag which of the following?');
VALUES ('fdfb2293-b0f9-4b99-aa1a-d780d6ed93ab', '34939af2-61d5-470b-887e-4f39fab7820b', 'A genuine query opportunity exists when objective clinical evidence suggests a condition of coding/severity significance that has not yet been specifically or consistently documented — not when documentation is already clear and complete.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('a24c6a1a-a46c-4cc5-9298-dd4397e7f58e', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 70, 'A retrospective query (submitted after discharge, before final billing) asking a provider to clarify a diagnosis based on clinical indicators already present in the discharge record is:');
VALUES ('a24c6a1a-a46c-4cc5-9298-dd4397e7f58e', '1a7e0233-2110-420e-8e85-41331ed092b8', 'Retrospective (post-discharge, pre-bill) queries are a standard, permitted part of many query programs and must meet the same non-leading, indicator-based compliance standard as concurrent (during-stay) queries — timing alone doesn''t make a query compliant or non-compliant.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

-- ── Section: Domain 4 — Regulatory Compliance (20 questions) — Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts. ──

VALUES ('a9cb0c68-f1da-4739-ad6b-959d7500d04c', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 71, 'The HIPAA Privacy Rule primarily governs:');
VALUES ('a9cb0c68-f1da-4739-ad6b-959d7500d04c', 'cd4dbef5-c543-4bf2-a149-d5ee0072296d', 'The Privacy Rule addresses use/disclosure of PHI broadly (any form — paper, electronic, oral); the HIPAA Security Rule specifically addresses safeguards for electronic PHI (ePHI).', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('23bb3fab-be82-4ceb-8863-8f48cbd2dbd5', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 72, 'Which of the following best distinguishes the HIPAA Security Rule from the Privacy Rule?');
VALUES ('23bb3fab-be82-4ceb-8863-8f48cbd2dbd5', '4878eada-d9f9-492a-bda2-f087a900dc88', 'Security Rule = safeguards (administrative/physical/technical) for ePHI specifically; Privacy Rule = broader use/disclosure standards for PHI in any format.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('ce41a10e-3528-4842-8461-1ab8b19657ef', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 73, 'A Patient Safety Indicator (PSI) is best described as:');
VALUES ('ce41a10e-3528-4842-8461-1ab8b19657ef', 'd85777be-29d8-4be2-a6ec-ae98e2975f3a', 'PSIs (an AHRQ Quality Indicator set) use coded administrative data — including POA indicators — to flag potentially preventable in-hospital complications, which is why accurate coding (especially POA) directly affects PSI measurement.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('269bf9f2-8448-4c7b-8e78-fa4f953d0981', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 74, 'A Hospital-Acquired Condition (HAC), for Medicare payment policy purposes, refers to a condition that:');
VALUES ('269bf9f2-8448-4c7b-8e78-fa4f953d0981', '2695ceb0-d1f3-4b3d-ac42-74a2621e3ffb', 'HACs are conditions not present on admission that CMS has identified as reasonably preventable — accurate POA indicator assignment is the coding mechanism that identifies whether a condition should be evaluated under the HAC payment provisions.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('2cc0f461-b78f-4ceb-b160-b36cddfd18c2', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 75, 'Per AHIMA''s Standards of Ethical Coding, a coder who is asked by a supervisor to assign a higher-weighted code without supporting clinical documentation should:');
VALUES ('2cc0f461-b78f-4ceb-b160-b36cddfd18c2', '55a888ba-1c27-4dd8-9f0d-956845356fa3', 'AHIMA''s ethical standards obligate coders to code strictly based on documented, supported clinical information — a request to inflate coding without support should be declined and escalated via compliance channels, not carried out.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('8a009f7e-1654-4434-8f36-732bb7c55f89', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 76, 'UHDDS compliance in the coding process most directly requires:');
VALUES ('8a009f7e-1654-4434-8f36-732bb7c55f89', 'a12cb2fb-ff1a-4cdd-adf0-df5be58b0cb8', 'UHDDS defines a uniform minimum data set (principal diagnosis, additional diagnoses, principal/significant procedures, and more) that must be applied consistently for inpatient discharge reporting, regardless of payer, as the basis for standardized national data.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('86c28deb-1c37-4bc5-880f-0af35ea1a614', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 77, 'Which of the following is an example of ensuring compliance with HIPAA''s ''minimum necessary'' standard in a coding department?');
VALUES ('86c28deb-1c37-4bc5-880f-0af35ea1a614', 'd8177598-676f-4418-a9fa-6f063a0ce834', 'The ''minimum necessary'' standard requires limiting PHI access/use/disclosure to what is reasonably necessary for the specific purpose — including internal staff access controls, not just external disclosures.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('de246ceb-ea06-4a04-b715-0f84789452a7', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 78, 'A facility''s HIM compliance program identifies a pattern of upcoding (assigning higher-severity diagnosis codes than clinical documentation supports) to increase reimbursement. This raises exposure under which federal law, among others?');
VALUES ('de246ceb-ea06-4a04-b715-0f84789452a7', '6354367d-b155-45e7-800e-9fb029d02a97', 'Systematic upcoding that results in claims submitted to federal healthcare programs (Medicare/Medicaid) that don''t match documented clinical reality creates False Claims Act exposure — a core reason coding accuracy and ethical query practice matter so much in this domain.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('7454fd88-0b4f-4c70-888a-5be09f0a1485', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 79, 'Ensuring ''completeness and accuracy of health records'' for regulatory compliance purposes includes verifying that:');
VALUES ('7454fd88-0b4f-4c70-888a-5be09f0a1485', 'bc68e557-03bb-40eb-86ae-61d06f0021ab', 'Completeness/accuracy compliance spans authentication, timeliness, and the presence of documentation genuinely supporting all coded diagnoses and procedures — not just a superficially complete billing summary.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('a0af236a-f28e-42ee-bc74-df9a49a93859', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 80, 'Which best describes the general purpose of the HITECH Act as it relates to health information compliance?');
VALUES ('a0af236a-f28e-42ee-bc74-df9a49a93859', 'a168bfe7-7f82-4611-905c-7fd623ebc438', 'HITECH strengthened HIPAA privacy/security enforcement (notably introducing breach notification requirements) while also incentivizing EHR adoption and meaningful use — both aspects are relevant to CCS Domain 4/5 compliance knowledge.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('73bc9669-79fe-48a9-8b00-e2a8cf6df046', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 81, 'Under HIPAA''s breach notification requirements (as strengthened by HITECH), a covered entity that experiences a breach of unsecured PHI is generally required to:');
VALUES ('73bc9669-79fe-48a9-8b00-e2a8cf6df046', 'e78b4ddf-8897-4cd5-9657-7d79b83d5967', 'Breach notification rules require notifying affected individuals (and HHS, with additional media notification for larger breaches) within defined timeframes after discovery — a compliance obligation coding/HIM staff should understand given their PHI access.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('a3a94e71-9233-4dd2-88de-f91ca70fca15', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 82, 'A payer-specific coding/billing guideline conflicts with a general ICD-10-CM Official Guideline in a way that would result in a less accurate code being submitted to that payer. The generally correct compliance approach is:');
VALUES ('a3a94e71-9233-4dd2-88de-f91ca70fca15', '1220519e-15c1-4e55-819d-9a26eaa66589', 'Payer-specific guidance can affect billing mechanics (documentation requirements, modifiers, claim edits) but does not override the obligation to code accurately per the Official Guidelines — knowingly submitting an inaccurate code to satisfy a payer preference is a compliance risk, not an accepted practice.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('5d2d5446-7753-4cc0-99fa-b566504f012d', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 83, 'The National Correct Coding Initiative (NCCI) edits are primarily designed to:');
VALUES ('5d2d5446-7753-4cc0-99fa-b566504f012d', 'e6c269b9-419a-461f-ac92-335040efeaf5', 'NCCI edits (Procedure-to-Procedure and Medically Unlikely Edits) target improper payment from unbundling and other incorrect code-pair combinations — a core regulatory-compliance concept tested under CCS Domain 1 (coding edits) and Domain 4 (regulatory compliance) alike.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('efbdc7f2-253c-4ced-9682-6ee6d7925124', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 84, 'AHIMA''s Standards of Ethical Coding specifically address a coder''s obligation regarding query practices. Which best reflects that obligation?');
VALUES ('efbdc7f2-253c-4ced-9682-6ee6d7925124', '9d242346-08d5-468f-a79a-b39b482c3583', 'Ethical coding practice includes querying (compliantly, non-leading) when documentation genuinely doesn''t support confident, accurate code assignment — assumption-based coding in place of a reasonable query is itself an ethics/compliance concern.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('3fb22086-c3ed-42b5-bacd-4ec84f8d56dd', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 85, 'A compliance program''s internal auditing/monitoring function, as it relates to coding, primarily serves to:');
VALUES ('3fb22086-c3ed-42b5-bacd-4ec84f8d56dd', '4683c296-f011-426e-9036-f745ed376047', 'Ongoing internal auditing/monitoring is a core element of an effective compliance program (per OIG compliance program guidance), catching systemic coding-accuracy issues proactively rather than only after external audit or payer recoupment.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('a8d0489e-9ca8-4f10-a80e-43f49b750604', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 86, 'Under the False Claims Act, which of the following best describes ''knowingly'' submitting a false claim, in the context most relevant to coding compliance?');
VALUES ('a8d0489e-9ca8-4f10-a80e-43f49b750604', 'e2853167-4477-4f14-89d8-4aaa89d89585', 'The False Claims Act''s ''knowing'' standard includes reckless disregard/deliberate ignorance, not just provable specific intent — this is why systemic, unverified coding shortcuts (not just deliberate fraud) carry real compliance risk.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('43009811-d6b9-4e11-946e-4fe4fc080031', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 87, 'Which best describes the relationship between AHIMA''s Standards of Ethical Coding and a facility''s own internal coding compliance policy?');
VALUES ('43009811-d6b9-4e11-946e-4fe4fc080031', '2762ff69-cfdc-4b2e-abb6-d1ae4867d806', 'Credentialed coders (CCS holders) are professionally bound by AHIMA''s ethical standards regardless of employer, and a compliant facility''s internal policy should operationalize — not conflict with — that professional ethical baseline.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('8f685530-81b3-4c46-bc32-ecdff3ca11eb', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 88, 'Which scenario best illustrates a HIPAA Security Rule administrative safeguard, as opposed to a physical or technical safeguard?');
VALUES ('8f685530-81b3-4c46-bc32-ecdff3ca11eb', '630975ee-c8e5-4410-aed1-db2d94a7bd98', 'Administrative safeguards are the policies/procedures/workforce management side of security (risk assessment, training, access-authorization policy); physical safeguards address facility/device physical access (locked doors); technical safeguards address the technology itself (encryption, firewalls, access controls in software).', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('8baa0422-32f6-4d70-92a6-be844f408f37', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 89, 'A coding compliance plan''s ''corrective action'' component, following identification of a coding error pattern through internal audit, would most appropriately include:');
VALUES ('8baa0422-32f6-4d70-92a6-be844f408f37', '79f94644-9cc6-4737-8746-7c027aed53f8', 'Effective compliance programs respond to identified error patterns with targeted education and, where warranted, claim correction — proactive self-identification and correction is itself viewed favorably under OIG compliance guidance, versus ignoring a known pattern.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('b7d17a91-65a8-4923-bf33-c484cbce627e', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 90, 'Medical necessity coding edits are designed to verify that:');
VALUES ('b7d17a91-65a8-4923-bf33-c484cbce627e', '08b5e468-013a-49b8-85b6-30a225115821', 'Medical necessity edits check that the reported diagnosis code(s) support the reported service/procedure as reasonable and necessary per coverage policy (e.g., NCDs/LCDs for Medicare) — a mismatch is a common reason for denied claims, distinct from NCCI''s code-pair-bundling focus.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC program concepts.');

-- ── Section: Domain 5 — Information Technologies (10 questions) — Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH. ──

VALUES ('b5f41433-807e-4a7b-b424-4339c3fadfb0', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 91, 'An Electronic Health Record (EHR) is best distinguished from an Electronic Medical Record (EMR) by which characteristic?');
VALUES ('b5f41433-807e-4a7b-b424-4339c3fadfb0', 'c99d772a-22a6-49c9-8b37-780fd9c55e03', 'EMR traditionally refers to a single organization''s digital version of a paper chart; EHR is built around interoperability and information sharing across multiple organizations/providers involved in a patient''s care.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('d046833c-2598-45fc-be7e-b07b72f9720c', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 92, 'Encoder software in a coding department is primarily used to:');
VALUES ('d046833c-2598-45fc-be7e-b07b72f9720c', 'f1754ba5-c5d3-48c1-bde5-940f0decaeb6', 'Encoders are a coding-support tool (logic-based code lookup, sequencing prompts, edit-checking) — they assist and structure the coding process but do not eliminate the need for coder clinical/coding judgment based on the actual documentation.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('3e746a12-638a-47df-9e30-0add7ccd215f', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 93, 'Grouper software''s primary function in an inpatient HIM/coding workflow is to:');
VALUES ('3e746a12-638a-47df-9e30-0add7ccd215f', 'e98ea30b-a70a-41e2-8719-f6994a1c0b39', 'Grouper software applies DRG-assignment logic to coded diagnosis/procedure data (plus other case attributes like discharge disposition) to determine the appropriate MS-DRG or other grouping, which drives reimbursement and severity/quality reporting.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('50c1681b-0ee1-4d83-8adc-aea5f3745f10', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 94, 'Computer-Assisted Coding (CAC) software is best described as a tool that:');
VALUES ('50c1681b-0ee1-4d83-8adc-aea5f3745f10', '112752df-855b-42f2-b220-be18fd9cabb7', 'CAC uses NLP/pattern recognition to surface suggested codes from documentation, but industry and regulatory expectations (and accreditation/compliance best practice) require a qualified coder to review and validate CAC-suggested codes before finalization — CAC assists, it does not replace, coder judgment.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('bddf2ea6-2745-471d-849d-ce6276b33aee', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 95, 'A known risk of over-reliance on CAC-suggested codes without adequate coder review is:');
VALUES ('bddf2ea6-2745-471d-849d-ce6276b33aee', 'c27b470d-e36e-473a-b8e1-2fe50ab1a26d', 'Because CAC often relies on NLP pattern-matching against documentation text, it can miss clinical nuance, sequencing rules, POA context, or guideline exceptions — human coder review remains essential for compliance and accuracy.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('34c12f21-02b9-4a06-919a-4b27b8fb3a66', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 96, 'Which of the following best reflects a HITECH-related compliance consideration specific to HIM information technology systems?');
VALUES ('34c12f21-02b9-4a06-919a-4b27b8fb3a66', '3647f93a-1dd1-413e-8164-77ac6201aa48', 'Any system touching ePHI — including coding, abstracting, encoder, and CAC platforms — falls under HIPAA Security Rule obligations that HITECH reinforced (stronger enforcement, breach notification, meaningful-use-linked security requirements).', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('6850f5a0-b310-4577-bbb9-7a5168b04692', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 97, 'A ''legacy system'' interoperability challenge in HIM technology most often refers to:');
VALUES ('6850f5a0-b310-4577-bbb9-7a5168b04692', '0ae68f37-8342-4f3c-9d2b-55df7fa4de54', 'Legacy-system interoperability issues arise when older systems use data formats/structures incompatible with newer EHR/HIM platforms, complicating data exchange, migration, and coding workflows that span both.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('59a9b167-2b5a-458a-b532-18b24bd8f876', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 98, 'Which best describes the coder''s continued responsibility when using an encoder or CAC tool that flags a potential code based on documentation?');
VALUES ('59a9b167-2b5a-458a-b532-18b24bd8f876', 'ccc0908e-4f2d-477d-a95e-56186a8f3350', 'Regardless of the technology tool used, the coder retains professional responsibility for verifying that any suggested code is actually supported by the documentation and correctly applies coding guidelines — technology assists, but does not transfer, that accountability.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('7309e031-c940-45b7-b702-6f30f9f1b23e', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 99, 'In the context of HIM technology, ''interoperability'' most precisely means:');
VALUES ('7309e031-c940-45b7-b702-6f30f9f1b23e', '4eca0007-5546-4bf6-a57b-04a016a325ee', 'Interoperability is about the capacity for different systems/devices to exchange and meaningfully use data together — a foundational concept for EHR data exchange, health information exchanges (HIEs), and coding-adjacent system integration.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('91e0d7a8-94ec-42b0-8a03-a36aa5610a86', 'c25331b4-ba0d-40b2-9757-38e41b048f25', 100, 'Which scenario best illustrates a legitimate use of encoder/grouper software output as part of a compliant coding and DRG-assignment workflow?');
VALUES ('91e0d7a8-94ec-42b0-8a03-a36aa5610a86', '2ab16a2d-2ca1-45a2-a427-be5fbbb4a113', 'The compliant workflow is: documentation-driven coding (assisted, not replaced, by encoder logic) feeds into the grouper, which then calculates DRG from those verified codes — not the reverse (picking diagnoses to hit a target DRG, or skipping coder review of system output).', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR types, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');
